{"haunt":{"id":"63cc2c82-364e-419f-abd4-375a1dbfb3ce","word":"haunt","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"place at which one is regularly found","tags":["masculine"],"word":"poiso"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"place at which one is regularly found","tags":["masculine"],"word":"poiso habitual"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"place at which one is regularly found","tags":["masculine"],"word":"pouso"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"place at which one is regularly found","tags":["masculine"],"word":"pouso habitual"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"The shopping mall is a popular haunt of the local teenagers in this town.","bold_text_offsets":[[31,36]],"type":"example","id":"9d886670-656b-42e4-a786-37520c8637f8"},{"text":"I went back the town I used to live and visited all my old haunts.","bold_text_offsets":[[59,65]],"type":"example","id":"510b2f92-d50c-4830-85ed-da7b0a7f28d8"},{"text":"It is a great rock or cliff on the loneliest part of the mountains, and, … is known by the name of the Garden Rock. Near the foot of it is a small lake, the haunt of the solitary bittern, with water-snakes basking in the sun on the leaves of the pond-lilies which lie on the surface.","ref":"1819, Washington Irving, The Sketch Book, Rip Van Winkle:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[157,162]],"id":"79043003-b5e2-4a9f-bdf5-dd51a94cbb5c"},{"text":"Both Jack and Fletcher had graduated the year before, but still took an interest in their old haunts, and patronized the fellows who were not yet through.","ref":"1868, Louisa May Alcott, Kitty's Class Day:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[94,100]],"id":"0e29df7a-9174-41d0-ba8c-cf15316c879a"},{"text":"Wyoming has been a favorite haunt of paleontologists for the past century ever since westering pioneers reported that many vertebrate fossils were almost lying on the ground.","ref":"1984 October 8, Timothy Loughran, Natalie Angier, “Science: Striking It Rich in Wyoming”, in Time:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[28,33]],"id":"9e8e3106-132d-4d43-9621-a64baa76d5d0"},{"text":"It shall be the haunt of jackals, an abode for ostriches.","ref":"2018, Michael Coogan, Marc Brettler, Carol Newsom, The New Oxford Annotated Bible with Apocrypha:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[16,21]],"id":"e114c9d3-fe5b-472c-99f3-659f40a3af6b"}],"links":[["habitation","habitation"],["hangout","hangout"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"repair"}],"categories":["English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples","Quotation templates to be cleaned"],"glosses":["A place at which one is regularly found; a habitation or hangout."],"id":"bcc033bc-bdbd-46df-bc1c-3da52a488c8f"},{"categories":["English dialectal terms"],"links":[["ghost","ghost"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"haint"}],"raw_glosses":["(dialect) A ghost."],"glosses":["A ghost."],"tags":["dialectal"],"id":"270c1e84-6c2e-4893-afb0-f765ec9d7eb5"},{"examples":[{"text":"The lofty mountains roſe faint to the ſight and loſt their foreheads in the diſtant ſkies: the little hills, cloathed in darker green and ſkirted with embroidered vales, diſcovered the ſecret haunts of kids and bounding roes.","ref":"1769, Firishta, translated by Alexander Dow, Tales translated from the Persian of Inatulla of Delhi, volume I, Dublin: P. and W. Wilson et al., page iv:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[192,198]],"id":"23f049bf-ccd5-483b-8f01-9a979e800733"}],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"glosses":["A lair or feeding place of animals."],"id":"bbaa9f27-1713-463b-b046-19c06b61028f"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"haunted":{"id":"695aefea-b3e1-4388-8b08-dfc9c365b006","word":"haunted","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"frequented by a ghost","tags":["masculine"],"word":"assombrado"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"frequented by a ghost","tags":["masculine"],"word":"mal-assombrado"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"obsessed","word":"obcecado"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"showing a feeling of being disturbed","word":"perturbado"}],"pos":"adj","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"The hotel was haunted by a disembodied spirit.","bold_text_offsets":[[14,21]],"type":"example","id":"207ac7eb-2578-44b7-a8a0-87803386b32b"}],"links":[["frequent","frequent"],["ghost","ghost"]],"glosses":["Of a location, frequented by a ghost or ghosts."],"categories":["English terms with usage examples"],"id":"836f2e82-54e5-4121-be86-61143e764c6a"},{"examples":[{"text":"a soldier haunted by the memories of combat","id":"a31e55f3-7eb2-4d3c-a4fc-63e98fe1bda3"}],"glosses":["Obsessed (by an idea, threat, etc.)."],"id":"be123d58-70ae-4731-baec-6ee6b51ab9b3"},{"examples":[{"text":"a haunted expression","id":"73c3dfdd-7d62-4524-af80-e4e4e2c251fc"}],"links":[["disturbed","disturbed"]],"glosses":["Showing a feeling of being disturbed."],"id":"2a09c35e-32f4-4278-b82f-88f530e6e7f1"}],"related":[{"word":"cursed"}],"synonyms":[]},"haughty":{"id":"87ed192e-248a-4d86-ab24-46187bd6f28b","word":"haughty","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"disdainful, supercilious; in demeanour conveying the assumption of superiority","word":"soberbo"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"disdainful, supercilious; in demeanour conveying the assumption of superiority","word":"vanglorioso"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"disdainful, supercilious; in demeanour conveying the assumption of superiority","word":"imodesto"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"disdainful, supercilious; in demeanour conveying the assumption of superiority","word":"altivo"}],"pos":"adj","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"How meek and shrunken did that haughty Tarmac become as it slunk by the wide circle of asphalt of the yellow sort